        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Kohima</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">4 April to 22 June 1944, in three stages: the Japanese attack on Kohima ridge (3 to 16 April), the British and Indian counter-attack (18 April to 13 May) and the pursuit (16 May to 22 June)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Kohima ridge and the surrounding hills, now the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagaland">Nagaland</a> in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimapur">Dimapur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imphal">Imphal</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">British and British Indian forces, first the garrison under Colonel Hugh Richards (about 2,500, of whom about 1,000 were non-combatants) and then the British 2nd Division and Indian XXXIII Corps under Lieutenant General Montagu Stopford; the Japanese 31st Division under Lieutenant General Kōtoku Satō, part of Lieutenant General Renya Mutaguchi's Fifteenth Army</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Japanese abandoned Kohima ridge in May and the Naga Village in late May, and on 22 June troops from Kohima and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imphal">Imphal</a> met at Milestone 109, ending the Siege of Imphal. British and Indian forces lost 4,064 men dead, missing and wounded; the Japanese lost at least 5,764 battle casualties in the Kohima area.</span></div>
        </div>
